Package: os7-release
Version: @OS7_VERSION@
Architecture: all
Maintainer: Bastian Wirth <bastian.wirth@upinblue.com>
Section: admin
Priority: important
Depends: python3-minimal, base-files
Homepage: https://github.com/upinblue/os7
Description: OS/7 release identity, archive pin and trust anchor
 The package that says which OS/7 this is. It carries the release pin — the
 version, the Ubuntu archive snapshot and every component version and hash —
 the public key of OS/7's own package repository, the apt source that points at
 it, and the generator that brands /etc/os-release.
 .
 It DIVERTS /usr/lib/os-release away from base-files. That file is a conffile of
 a package which legitimately owns it, so before this package existed every apt
 run that touched base-files could revert the OS/7 branding, and the update
 sequence had to re-assert it as a step somebody had to remember forever. A
 divert makes it a mechanism instead: base-files can no longer write to that
 path at all, and a dpkg trigger re-derives the branded file whenever it tries.
 .
 The branding is deliberately narrow. PRETTY_NAME, IMAGE_ID, IMAGE_VERSION,
 VARIANT and the URLs are OS/7's; NAME, ID, ID_LIKE, VERSION, VERSION_ID and
 VERSION_CODENAME are left exactly as Ubuntu wrote them, because Microsoft's
 Intune agent and its Azure Arc onboarding script read three different subsets
 of them and one of them exits 133 on a NAME without "buntu" in it.
